### 🎤 Interview Answer — MediGrid AI

**“My project is MediGrid AI, an AI-powered prescription assistant.**

The main purpose of this project is to help users understand and manage their prescription information.

I built the frontend using **Streamlit** and the backend using **FastAPI**. I used **Google Gemini AI** to analyze prescription images and extract information such as the patient name, doctor name, medicine name, dosage, frequency, and duration.

I used **SQLite** to store prescription information and provide a prescription history feature.

The application also includes a **medicine warning system**, **pharmacy finder**, **location feature**, **AI chatbot**, and **PDF report generation**.

The basic workflow is:

**User uploads prescription → Streamlit sends the image to FastAPI → Gemini analyzes the image → prescription details are extracted → data can be saved in SQLite → user can view history, warnings, pharmacies, chatbot responses, and generate a PDF report.**

Through this project, I learned how to integrate **Generative AI with FastAPI, Streamlit, databases, APIs, and Python** into one complete application.”**


If they ask: “What was your role?”
**“I worked on the complete application, including the Streamlit frontend, FastAPI backend, Gemini AI integration, SQLite database, prescription extraction, chatbot, warning system, pharmacy feature, and PDF generation.”**


If they ask: “What was the biggest challenge?”
**“The main challenge was integrating different components together. For example, the Streamlit frontend had to communicate correctly with the FastAPI backend, and the Gemini response had to be converted into structured prescription data. I solved this by creating separate Python modules for each feature and connecting them through FastAPI endpoints.”**

